WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:14.085 --> 00:00:23.104
Court officer: Judgment in the matter of the&nbsp;
Prudential Assurance Company v Commissioners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.104 --> 00:00:24.720
for His Majesty s Revenue and Customs.
Lord Lloyd-Jones: Lady Simler will explain the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:31.200
judgment, which was handed down on 11 September.
Lady Simler: This appeal concerns the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:38.880
between section 43 of the Value Added Tax Act,&nbsp;
which lays down rules about VAT groups and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.360
provisions, both European and domestic, that&nbsp;
deal with the time at which services or goods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:49.200
are treated as supplied for VAT purposes.
Section 43 allows companies to choose to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.040
treated for VAT purposes as members of an entity&nbsp;
known as a VAT group. Once a VAT group is formed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.160
the business carried on by the members of the&nbsp;
group is treated for VAT purposes as carried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.800
on by the appointed representative member,&nbsp;
and supplies of goods and services between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.680
members of the group are disregarded so that&nbsp;
no VAT is payable on the intra group supplies.&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.480
The question in this case, is how the VAT group&nbsp;
disregard applies when between the time services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.640
were performed and the time VAT invoices for&nbsp;
those services were issued, the supplier left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.800
the VAT group. The answer depends on the proper&nbsp;
understanding of the effect of the time of supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:38.960
rules, set out in the Principal VAT Directive, the&nbsp;
VAT Act, and regulation 90 of the Value Added Tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:46.800
Regulations 1995. The VAT Act provides that VAT&nbsp;
on any supply of goods or services becomes due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:53.040
at the time of supply. The general rule is that&nbsp;
a supply of services is treated as taking place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.200
at the time when the services are performed.&nbsp;
There are exceptions, most relevantly here:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.840
section 6 subsection 14, and regulation 90 of the&nbsp;
Regulations together provide that where services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.960
are supplied over a period, and the whole or&nbsp;
part of the payment is made periodically or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.760
from time to time, the supply is taken to&nbsp;
occur at the time when the supplier issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.080
a VAT invoice or when payment is received.
In this case, a company called Silverfleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:28.320
performed fund management services for Prudential,&nbsp;
when they were both members of the same VAT group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.360
At the time the services were performed by&nbsp;
Silverfleet, and when Prudential paid the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:42.000
management fees that were due, those supplies were&nbsp;
properly disregarded for VAT purposes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.120
years later, Silverfleet stopped managing&nbsp;
funds for Prudential and left the VAT group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.600
and at that point, success fees were triggered&nbsp;
when the value of the funds exceeded a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:59.920
contractual hurdle rate and Silverfleet&nbsp;
issued invoices for it in 2015 and 2016.&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.120
The issue in the appeal is whether VAT is payable&nbsp;
on the success fees because Prudential and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:11.280
Silverfleet were no longer in the same VAT group&nbsp;
when Silverfleet invoiced Prudential for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.520
or whether the fees are not subject to&nbsp;
VAT because they arose out of services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:22.000
that were fully performed and in that sense,&nbsp;
completed while they were in the same group.&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.480
HMRC decided that VAT was properly payable. The&nbsp;
First-tier Tribunal allowed Prudential's appeal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:34.320
but that decision was reversed by the Upper&nbsp;
Tribunal and the majority of the Court of Appeal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.320
The Supreme Court dismisses this appeal. Lady Rose&nbsp;
and I give the judgment with which Lords Reed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.640
Hodge, Lloyd-Jones, Stephens, and Richards&nbsp;
agree. We sat as a panel of seven because one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.200
of the arguments put forward by Prudential&nbsp;
invited the Supreme Court to disagree with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:57.440
three earlier decisions of the House of Lords if&nbsp;
those decisions couldn't be distinguished. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:03.040
judgment, we discussed the three House of Lords&nbsp;
decisions in detail and summarise their effect.&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.240
We conclude, in agreement with the Court of&nbsp;
Appeal, that section 43 of the VAT Act is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:14.720
a complete code with its own time of supply rules.&nbsp;
Instead, it must be read with section 6 of the Act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:20.720
and the Regulations to determine when a supply&nbsp;
between members of the same VAT group took place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.600
The Court of Appeal was therefore&nbsp;
correct to apply regulation 90 sub one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.240
in order to determine whether the supplies&nbsp;
that triggered the success fees are treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.680
as having taken place while Silverfleet&nbsp;
was inside or outside the VAT group.&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.720
We conclude that the reasoning of the House of&nbsp;
Lords in the three earlier cases supports that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.280
outcome. On this basis, regulation 90 sub one&nbsp;
has the effect of splitting the supplies made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.000
by Silverfleet that are ongoing or that attract&nbsp;
successive payments into a series of separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.920
successive supplies, and each successive supply&nbsp;
by Silverfleet is treated as different in time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.520
from the one before. In other words, the timing&nbsp;
of the supplies to which the success fees relate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.960
is modified by the successive payments of&nbsp;
the success fees so that the services are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:18.160
treated as having been supplied at the time the&nbsp;
invoice was issued or at the time of payment.&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:24.640
Given that conclusion, which was based on domestic&nbsp;
law only, the judgment went on to consider a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:31.440
argument raised by Prudential. Namely, whether&nbsp;
regulation 90, as so applied, is a permissible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:41.200
domestic implementation of Article 64 or 66 of the&nbsp;
Directive as those articles have been interpreted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:47.280
by decisions of the Court of Justice of the&nbsp;
European Union, decisions which predate the UK's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:53.600
withdrawal from the EU. Prudential's case is that&nbsp;
neither article can be relied on as permitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:06:01.120
Regulation 90 to be applied in this way. We&nbsp;
agree with Prudential that Article 66 cannot be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.920
relied on because it only gives member states the&nbsp;
option to postpone the time of collection of VAT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:11.120
to the time of invoice or payment. It doesn't&nbsp;
alter or affect the time of the chargeable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:19.040
event. The question is therefore, whether the&nbsp;
modification made by Regulation 90 sub one to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:26.720
the time at which the chargeable event occurs&nbsp;
is a permissible implementation of Article 64.&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:33.280
We hold in the judgment that it is. The wording of&nbsp;
Article 64 is wide, and it does have the effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:40.320
modifying the time of the chargeable event,&nbsp;
as well as the time of chargeability of VAT.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.080
The judgment recognises that Article 64 has been&nbsp;
interpreted restrictively by the Court of Justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:52.800
in a number of cases. We discuss those cases.&nbsp;
However, consistently with those cases, we hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:59.680
that Article 64 can apply where the reason why&nbsp;
the supply of services gives rise to a successive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.760
payment is because, as in the present case, the&nbsp;
parties to the contract do not know at the date&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.760
when the services are performed or when continuous&nbsp;
performance of the services comes to an end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:18.000
whether any further payment will ever become due&nbsp;
or, if it does, in what amount. In this case, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:24.000
only became clear that success fees were payable&nbsp;
when the contractual hurdle rate was crossed. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:30.400
was after Silverfleet left the VAT group. In&nbsp;
other words, Article 64 applies to supplies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:37.040
the nature of which justify payment in instalments&nbsp;
and can include situations where the payment for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.040
services is uncertain or contingent at the time&nbsp;
the performance of the services is completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.960
and where the successive payment comprises an&nbsp;
element of payment that crystallises only once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:54.960
the uncertainty or contingency is resolved.
Here, the payment of the success fees by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:02.480
Prudential to Silverfleet was a successive payment&nbsp;
within the meaning of Article 64. Accordingly, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:08.080
answer to the question whether the services were&nbsp;
performed for VAT purposes at the time Silverfleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:14.080
was in the same group as Prudential is no, so far&nbsp;
as the success fees are concerned, because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:20.400
chargeable event to which the payment or invoices&nbsp;
for those fees relate was modified or postponed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:31.342
to the extent of the value of the services&nbsp;
reflected in the later 2015 and 2016 invoices.

00:08:31.342 --> 00:08:34.800
Lord Lloyd-Jones: Thank you.

